Author: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Thu Sep 24 02:52:20 2026 +0000

    Compiled ZZ32 and ZZ64 shifts saturate and reverse, zero no longer overflows, narrow keeps the low 32 bits

    Pavol's integer rules of 2026-09-22 on the compiled path (climb batch 3.5,
    rung B). LSHIFT/RSHIFT on ZZ32 and ZZ64 call four natives with the
    smart-shift rule: a count at or beyond the width gives 0 or the sign, a
    negative count shifts the other way. The sixteen x == -x guards of
    simpleIntArith/simpleLongArith are gone, twelve testing the minimum and the
    two long multiplies using Math.multiplyExact, so |0|, -0, 0 DIV -1,
    DOTMINUS 0 and zero factors answer 0, ZZ64 REM/MOD/GCD/LCM no longer throw
    on a zero quotient, and a product equal to the minimum is answered.
    ZZ64.narrow and NN64.narrow keep the low 32 bits; rung W's five throw
    assertions are reversed. Gated by compiler_tests/IntSemanticsRungB.

    Rows 333 and 378 closed, 335 and 346 closed for the compiled half, row 379
    opened against walk's silent wrap with its XXX home owed. REPORT.md,
    record.md and SKEPTIC.md are written at the gather from the structured
    results the harness kept from being files; the skeptic's seven corrections
    are made, the DOTCROSS messages re-cited and the test re-run.

